 ## Background
-The use of hierarchical data structures for volume rendering originated with the need to handle large, sparse datasets efficiently. Early works focused on octree-based traversal to skip empty space [S1]. The specific encoding of octrees into 3D textures for GPU acceleration was developed to leverage hardware texture sampling for pointer chasing [S2, unofficial]. Hybrid CPU-GPU approaches for AMR (Adaptive Mesh Refinement) data were introduced to handle perspective-correct rendering of non-uniform grids [S1]. Binary tree indexing for terrain patches was proposed to simplify view-dependent LOD management [S3]. Point-based octrees are widely used in general-purpose graphics libraries for spatial queries [S4, unofficial].
+The use of hierarchical data structures for volume rendering originated with the need to handle large, sparse datasets efficiently [G]. Early implementations utilized KD-tree hierarchies traversed on the CPU back-to-front for AMR patch rendering [S1]. Server-client architectures transfer gradient octrees computed on the server side to rendering clients to reduce network traffic and avoid client-side gradient computation [S2, unofficial]. Hybrid CPU-GPU approaches for AMR data handle perspective-correct rendering of non-uniform grids by modifying indices in the vertex shader [S1]. Binary tree indexing organizes terrain patches in a breadth-first manner to simplify view-dependent LOD management and decompression [S3]. Point-based octrees manage 3D points with recursive insertion, removal, and spatial queries like nearest neighbor and radius search [S4, unofficial].
 
